  "id": "Compound::DB01124 - Gene::3767",
  "verbalization": "Tolbutamide, a sulfonylurea antidiabetic agent, is implicated in its interaction with Kcnj11 through a series of mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Tolbutamide binds to Kcnj1, which is a component of the Atp-activated Inward Rectifier Potassium Channel, and this binding is linked to the activation of Kcnj11. This implies a direct or indirect interaction between Tolbutamide and the potassium channel subunit Kcnj11, potentially modulating its activity. The presence of multiple sulfonylurea compounds, including Tolbutamide, in the pathway further supports the classification of Tolbutamide as a member of the sulfonylurea class, which is known to interact with potassium channels in the context of glucose regulation.\n\nThe evidence from the three paths highlights a consistent mechanism where Tolbutamide, through its inclusion in sulfonylurea compounds, interacts with Kcnj11. The lowest common ancestors of Tolbutamide and its metabolites, such as Tolazamide and Glimepiride, all point to the N-Sulfonylurea and Sulfonylurea Antidiabetic Agent classes, reinforcing the pharmacological context of Tolbutamide. The final scores from the paths suggest a strong, though not absolute, association between Tolbutamide and Kcnj11, likely mediated through the modulation of potassium channel activity. This relationship is further supported by the presence of multiple pathways that converge on Kcnj11, indicating a potential role in cellular signaling and glucose homeostasis. The integration of these findings supports a mechanistic link between Tolbutamide and Kcnj11, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
